# Verification

`make check` is the fast local gate. It runs format checking, type-aware lint,
typechecking, layer, flag-expiry, and env-catalog checks, spelling, dead-code detection, script
tests, unit tests, and the web bundle budget.

It is not the full CI gate. CI runs overlapping checks in parallel and adds
history- and service-dependent work such as secret and commit scanning,
changesets, OpenAPI drift, container builds and scans, integration tests,
Playwright, Lighthouse, and CodeQL. Reproduce relevant paths with
`make openapi-check`, `make images`, `make test-integration`, `make e2e`, or
`make lighthouse`; some require Docker and `make deps-up-test`.

Faster individual loops: `make lint`, `make typecheck`, `make test`, `make layers`, `make env-catalog`.

Do not report work complete without running it. If a failure looks pre-existing,
confirm that on a clean tree instead of assuming.

## When a check fails, fix the cause

```jsonc
// ❌ BAD — the check was the only thing keeping this correct
{ "rules": { "typescript/no-floating-promises": "off" } }
```

Disabling a rule, loosening a type, adding an ignore entry, or skipping a test is
a change to the repo's guarantees. It needs its own justification and review, and
usually an ADR. If you genuinely believe a rule is wrong here, say so and explain
why rather than turning it off quietly.

## Evidence over inference

Reading code tells you what it should do. Running it tells you what it does. When
reporting a fix, say what you ran and what came back — and prefer a failing test
that now passes over a description of why the change should work.

## Commits

Conventional Commits, enforced by commitlint: `feat(scope): summary`. Run
`pnpm changeset` when a `packages/*` public API changes.
